course: dictionary
lesson: 12559
title: Devour: eat, read or destroy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To devour something means to take it in quickly and eagerly, often until it is all gone. The word is strong, so it makes a picture in your head. «The hungry dog devoured its dinner in one minute.» The dog did not nibble. It ate everything very fast.
explain: Devour can also be used for reading or watching. «Sam devoured the whole book in a single weekend.» Sam could not stop reading, so the book was taken in quickly and eagerly, just like food. When you meet devour, ask: is someone eating, reading, or is something being destroyed?
explain: Devour can even describe a feeling. «She was devoured by jealousy when her friend won the prize.» Jealousy did not eat her, but it filled her up completely, as if it had taken over. Check the clues in the sentence to find out which meaning fits.
fact: Devour means to eat something quickly and hungrily, often all of it.
fact: «Devour» can also mean to read or watch something eagerly and quickly, as in «devour books».
fact: «Devour» can mean to destroy something completely, as in «The fire devoured the forest».
fact: When a feeling devours someone, it fills them completely, as in «devoured by jealousy».
fact: Devour is a verb. Its forms are devour, devours, devoured and devouring.
exercise: «The hungry dog devoured its dinner in one minute.» What does devoured mean here? | *Ate quickly and hungrily | Looked at it carefully | Carried it to the garden | Bought it from the shop
exercise: What does devoured mean in «Sam devoured the whole book in a single weekend»? | Wrote a review for it | Put it neatly on a shelf | *Read eagerly and quickly | Lost, and left behind on a bus
exercise: «The fire devoured the old barn.» Which meaning fits best? | Built it again slowly | Painted it bright red | *Destroyed it completely | Moved it into a field
test: «Jealousy devoured her heart.» What does devoured mean here? | *Filled her completely, as if it had taken over | Helped her to fall asleep | Left her alone for a while | Made her smile all day
test: Which word is closest in meaning to devour when it means eat quickly? | Whisper | *Gobble | Sleep | Stroll
exam: Your friend says devour is just another word for eat. Is that always true? | No, it means to sleep | Yes, it always means exactly the same as eat | Yes, but only in fairy tales | *Not always: devour can mean eat fast, but it can also mean read eagerly or destroy completely
